{#-
  DERIVED, not from upstream. TRACE addresses history compression only and
  publishes no observation prompt. This template keeps TRACE's own selection rule
  verbatim because it is exactly the right rule for this axis:

      "For each candidate fact ask: will a later step read this and act
       differently? If no, drop it."

  and keeps its list of what must survive: exact literals the agent will paste
  into a call. TRACE's history format is a working-state summary; an observation
  refinement is the same judgement applied to one API reply, so the criteria carry
  over unchanged while the output shape has to differ.
-#}
<observation>
{{ observation }}
</observation>

<current-task>
{{ task }}
</current-task>

<work-so-far>
{{ history }}
</work-so-far>

The observation above is a raw API reply that is too long to keep. Reduce it.

Treat it as evidence for the remaining steps, not as a record. For each candidate
fact ask: 'will a later step read this and act differently?' If no, drop it.

Preserve exact literals the agent will paste into a call: identifiers and the ids a
later call takes as arguments, exact amounts, exact timestamps, exact file paths,
exact API parameter names that differ from a naive guess, unexpired tokens, and any
error message or status code. After values are derived (counts, totals, ids,
classifications), carry the result rather than the inputs.

Drop the scaffolding: schema and field descriptions, prompt echoes, repeated
boilerplate, entries already stated in <work-so-far>, and intermediate values that
have been summarised. State a fact once. Never duplicate across the two output
sections.

Use this EXACT format:

# Reasoning
[For the entries you dropped, the test you applied: what would the next step have done differently if it had seen them]

# Refined Observation
[The reduced observation: what the next call needs, with exact literals]
